; 23-case.kurt

; `case` is `assume` with case distinctions in mind: several `case` blocks in
; a row, each assuming one alternative, combine into "or-elim" -- if you can
; reach the same goal from every case of a disjunction, you've proved the
; goal outright.
;
; two things must already be in scope for that combination to actually fire -- `case` itself
; does NOT check either one, or that your cases are exhaustive at all:
;   1. the disjunction fact itself (`A or B` below, `use`d up front)
;   2. an explicit restatement of the goal *after* the case blocks close (line 20 below) --
;      closing the last `case` does not automatically finish the or-elim for you
; forgetting either one just fails with an ordinary "can not derive" on that final line, not
; a `case`-specific error.

load prop
bool A, B, C
use A or B
use A implies C
use B implies C

show C
proof
    case A
        C
    case B
        C
    C
qed
